WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.229 --> 00:00:06.629
Hello, my name is Edward Bennett, and I'm an instructor here at Epic Games.

2
00:00:06.629 --> 00:00:13.810
I'd like to welcome you to this course, Introduction to Runtime Animation for Games.

3
00:00:13.810 --> 00:00:17.410
In this course, we focus on four key objectives.

4
00:00:17.410 --> 00:00:24.890
First, we'll understand how the animation blueprint serves as a framework for managing the character animation.

5
00:00:25.350 --> 00:00:33.189
And we'll explore the structure and learn how they interact with the other animation assets to control character movement.

6
00:00:33.189 --> 00:00:37.909
Next, we want you to be able to create an animation blueprint from scratch.

7
00:00:37.909 --> 00:00:45.710
This involves setting up state machines, variables, and nodes that define the specific animation behaviour and transitions.

8
00:00:45.710 --> 00:00:50.149
Our third objective is to trigger animations from within a blueprint.

9
00:00:50.149 --> 00:00:54.750
So we'll look at how to initiate animations based on a gameplay event.

10
00:00:54.789 --> 00:00:56.880
Like a key press.

11
00:00:56.880 --> 00:01:04.680
Lastly, we'll work on splitting a skeletal mesh, which will allow us to target specific sections of the mesh.

12
00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:12.769
This is crucial for animating parts of the character independently, such as waving hands or nodding a head.

13
00:01:12.769 --> 00:01:22.049
Each of these objectives should build on the last, providing us with a good foundational understanding of animation control.

14
00:01:22.049 --> 00:01:29.650
In the next video, we will start by going over some basic concepts so that you can understand what we're working with.
